    "rendered_prompt": "<|im_start|>system\nYou are a helpful assistant.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nWhat is the units digit of $3^1 + 3^3 + 3^5 + 3^7 + \\ldots + 3^{2009}$?\n\nThink step by step inside <think> </think> tags, then give your final answer inside \\boxed{}.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nTo determine the units digit of the sum \\(3^1 + 3^3 + 3^5 + 3^7 + \\ldots + 3^{2009}\\), we first need to identify the pattern in the units digits of the powers of 3.\n\nLet's compute the first few powers of 3 and observe their units digits:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^1 &= 3 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 3)} \\\\\n3^2 &= 9 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 9)} \\\\\n3^3 &= 27 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 7)} \\\\\n3^4 &= 81 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 1)} \\\\\n3^5 &= 243 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 3)} \\\\\n3^6 &= 729 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 9)} \\\\\n3^7 &= 2187 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 7)} \\\\\n3^8 &= 6561 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 1)} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nWe observe that the units digits repeat every 4 terms: 3, 9, 7, 1.\n\nNext, we need to determine how many terms in the sequence \\(3^1, 3^3, 3^5, 3^7, \\ldots, 3^{2009}\\) there are. This sequence is an arithmetic sequence of odd numbers starting from 1 and ending at 2009. The number of terms in this sequence can be found using the formula for the \\(n\\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence:\n\\[\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d\n\\]\nwhere \\(a_n = 2009\\), \\(a_1 = 1\\), and \\(d = 2\\). Solving for \\(n\\):\n\\[\n2009 = 1 + (n-1) \\cdot 2 \\\\\n2008 = (n-1) \\cdot 2 \\\\\n1004 = n-1 \\\\\nn = 1005\n\\]\nSo, there are 1005 terms in the sequence.\n\nNow, we need to find the units digit of the sum of these terms. Since the units digits repeat every 4 terms, we can group the terms into sets of 4:\n\\[\n(3 + 9 + 7 + 1) \\times \\left(\\frac{1005}{4}\\right)\n\\]\nFirst, we compute the sum of one complete cycle of 4 terms:\n\\[\n3 + 9 + 7 + 1 = 20\n\\]\nThe units digit of 20 is 0. Since 1005 is not a multiple of 4, we need to consider the remaining terms. The number of complete cycles of 4 terms is:\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1005}{4} \\right\\rfloor = 251\n\\]\nThe units digit of the sum of these 251 cycles is:\n\\[\n0 \\times 251 = 0\n\\]\nNow, we need to add the units digit of the remaining terms. The remaining terms are the first 1 term of the next cycle (since \\(1005 \\mod 4 = 1\\)):\n\\[\n3^1 = 3 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 3)}\n\\]\nThus, the units digit of the sum of the remaining terms is 3.\n\nFinally, we add the units digits of the complete cycles and the remaining terms:\n\\[\n0 + 3 = 3\n\\]\nTherefore, the units digit of \\(3^1 + 3^3 + 3^5 + 3^7 + \\ldots + 3^{2009}\\) is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nReview the solution above. Identify and correct any errors; if it is correct, retain its answer. Give your final answer inside \\boxed{}.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n",
